--- !u!114 &202114717
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 202114715}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9541d86e2fd84c1d9990edf0852d74ab, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.TextMeshPro::TMPro.TextMeshPro
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "<size=70%>Dirt, stains, wet patches and snow are layers on top of a surface's material, placed by a mask. There are three ways to add them, one scene each: inside the Lit shader (Weathering-Material), with decals (Weathering-Decals), and with a shader that mixes two surfaces by a mask (Weathering-Shaders).</size>\n\n<b>Weathering-Material \xB7 Inside the Lit shader</b>   <color=#F2C14E>\xB7 You are here</color>\n<size=70%>1 The Detail Mask \xB7 2 Darker or lighter \xB7 3 Occlusion and the light \xB7 4 Weathering states</size>\n\n<b>Weathering-Decals \xB7 Decals</b>\n<size=70%><color=#B8B8B8>1 A decal is a box \xB7 2 Decals on a wall \xB7 3 A poster on rough concrete \xB7 4 A puddle of smoothness \xB7 5 Rain ripples \xB7 6 Edge wear \xB7 7 Exercise: Weather a kit piece</color></size>\n\n<b>Weathering-Shaders \xB7 Shaders that mix by a mask</b>\n<size=70%><color=#B8B8B8>1 Wet: darker and smoother \xB7 2 Puddles: a mask and a level \xB7 3 Snow on what faces up \xB7 4 Damp from the ground</color></size>"
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 2f7116f10747a67409388e93052ae222, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: -6055507529455485361, guid: 2f7116f10747a67409388e93052ae222, type: 2}
  m_fontSharedMaterials: []
  m_fontMaterial: {fileID: 0}
